69	```
70	
71	## Components of the Server
72	
73	### Tools
74	
75	#### Tensor storage
76	- ```create_tensor```: Creates a new tensor based on a given name, shape, and values, and adds it to the tensor store. For the purposes of this server, tensors are vectors and matrices.
77	- ```view_tensor```: Display the contents of a tensor from the store .
78	- ```delete_tensor```: Deletes a tensor based on its name in the tensor store.
79	
80	#### Linear Algebra
81	- ```add_matrices```: Adds two matrices with the provided names, if compatible.
82	- ```subtract_matrices```: Subtracts two matrices with the provided names, if compatible.
83	- ```multiply_matrices```: Multiplies two matrices with the provided names, if compatible.
84	- ```scale_matrix```: Scales a matrix of the provided name by a certain factor, in-place by default.
85	- ```matrix_inverse```: Computes the inverse of the matrix with the provided name.
86	- ```transpose```: Computes the transpose of the inverse of the matrix of the provided name.
87	- ```determinant```: Computes the determinant of the matrix of the provided name.
88	- ```rank```: Computes the rank (number of pivots) of the matrix of the provided name.
89	- ```compute_eigen```: Calculates the eigenvectors and eigenvalues of the matrix of the provided name.
90	- ```qr_decompose```: Computes the QR factorization of the matrix of the provided name. The columns of Q are an orthonormal basis for the image of the matrix, and R is upper triangular.
91	- ```svd_decompose```: Computes the Singular Value Decomposition of the matrix of the provided name.
92	- ```find_orthonormal_basis```: Finds an orthonormal basis for the matrix of the provided name. The vectors returned are all pair-wise orthogonal and are of unit length.
93	- ```change_basis```: Computes the matrix of the provided name in the new basis.
94	
95	#### Vector Calculus
96	- ```vector_project```: Projects a vector in the tensor store to the specified vector in the same vector space
97	- ```vector_dot_product```: Computes the dot product of two vectors in the tensor stores based on their provided names.
98	- ```vector_cross_product```: Computes the cross product of two vectors in the tensor stores based on their provided names.
99	- ```gradient```: Computes the gradient of a multivariable function based on the input function. Example call: ```gradient("x^2 + 2xyz + zy^3")```. Do NOT include the function name (like f(x, y, z) = ...`).
100	- ```curl```: Computes the curl of a vector field based on the input vector field. The input string must be formatted as a python list. Example call: ```curl("[3xy, 2z^4, 2y]"")```.
101	- ```divergence```Computes the divergence of a vector field based on the input vector field. The input string must be formatted as a python list. Example call: ```divergence("[3xy, 2z^4, 2y]"")```.
102	- ```laplacian```Computes the laplacian of a scalar function (as the divergence of the gradient) or a vector field (where a component-wise laplacian is computed). If a scalar function is the input, it must be input in the same format as in the ```gradient``` tool. If the input is a vector field, it must be input in the same manner as the ```curl/divergence``` tools.
103	- ```directional_deriv```: Computes the directional derivative of a function in a given direction ```u``` By default, the tool normalizes ```u``` before computing the directional derivative, as specified by the ```unit``` parameter.
104	
105	#### Visualization
106	- ```plot_vector_field```: Plots a vector field (specified in the same format as in the curl/divergence functions). Currently, only 3d vector fields are supported. A 2d png perspective image of the vector field is returned. By default, the bounds of the graph are from -1 to 1 on each axis.
107	- ```plot_function```: Plots a function in 2d or 3d (based on the input variables), specified in the same format as in the ```gradient``` tool. Only the variables x and y can be used.
108	

